Fix CVE-2026-18103 print_hw_addr buffer overflow

ISC DHCP accepts hardware addresses up to HARDWARE_ADDR_LEN bytes (20),
including InfiniBand addresses.  print_hw_addr() has a 49-byte static buffer,
which is only sufficient for a colon-separated 16-byte address.  Formatting a
20-byte address therefore writes past the end of the buffer.

Size the buffer from HARDWARE_ADDR_LEN and reject lengths which cannot be
represented by struct hardware.  This preserves output for all valid supported
hardware addresses and prevents both over-read and overflow for an invalid
length supplied by a caller.

diff --git a/common/print.c b/common/print.c
index 3ed4c34..d6d2f52 100644
--- a/common/print.c
+++ b/common/print.c
@@ -173,11 +173,11 @@ char *print_hw_addr (htype, hlen, data)
 	const int hlen;
 	const unsigned char *data;
 {
-	static char habuf [49];
+	static char habuf [HARDWARE_ADDR_LEN * 3];
 	char *s;
 	int i;
 
-	if (hlen <= 0)
+	if ((hlen <= 0) || (hlen > HARDWARE_ADDR_LEN))
 		habuf [0] = 0;
 	else {
 		s = habuf;
